4|0@DebateAChristian^1440296871#cucffqq:PattycakeMills$:I separate knowledge (truths) into two types: absolute and necessary. Necessary truths are conclusions we come up using our 5 senses, which we know can be deceiving. But in order to function normally, it's necessary that I accept these things as truth. An absolute truth is something proven philosophically, and I would argue there's only two absolute truths. 1) I exist (how else could I think about existing if I didn't exist?).... 2) I think (I am thinking about existing) Assuming there only these two absolute truths, God, in any definition, can never be proven absolutely. So any sightings of huge beings with special powers does not absolutely prove that the being is real, or that it is God. However, depending on one's definition of God, it could be proven as a necessary truth. For instance, I sometimes imagine "God" could simply be the unconscious force that is otherwise known as "nature". We've observed, with our physical senses, certain patterns in the universe. Those patterns, also known as "science", are necessary truths from which we use to make decisions in our personal lives, as well as when building societies, or going to war. If I define God as "nature", then yes I know there's a god, but it's only a necessary truth. Let's say a giant hand reaches down from the sky and a booming voice says it's God and attempts to prove it by displaying special powers. Let's say this being is able to fly, or manipulate reality, or time. One could make the case that this is evidence that this being is God. It wouldn't be an absolute truth, but if this being was mindblowingly powerful and it was functionally necessary to believe it was a god, then it would become a necessary truth. 
