1|0@DebateAChristian^1440354235#cud0mey:Mr-Toy$:Chances are, you were told as a little kid that you have to believe in God, as I was, and haven't been "open" to anything since. It can be a scary thing to find out something you believe in is wrong. I know it's scary to have to imagine that God might not be real,or that we could be all alone in the universe, but the evidence against a Christian god existing is substantial. The evidence for the existence of God is anyone willing to say "well I think he is real and you can't change my mind because I think so." There's no one in your head talking to you or telling you to do things. That's you talking and wishing things to yourself. You would also think that if God was real that people who believed in God or lived a good Christian life would live better lives than the rest of us. But data in the U.S. taken in the most Christian parts of the state are actually doing worse than the average American. The Bible Belt of the U.S. Has higher obesity, higher diabetes, lower income, lower education levels, have a higher divorce rate, and consumes more gay porn than any other part of the country (not because there are more gay people there but because they are closeted. The thing I find most troubling about religious people (in my case Christians) they believe so strongly in something they, most times, know absolutely know anything about. Most Christians dedicate their entire life to a book they've never read. I was raised in a good natured, catholic family on a Midwestern farm. I grew up learning everything about the bible. I studied all sorts of religions in college and the historic births and impacts different religions had on the world. I'm in no way a know it all, I do consider myself open minded and experimental, and after my positive religious upbringing and my life's passion for understanding the existence of God in a religious sense and a philosophy sense, I am positive there is no evidence What so ever that God exists - especially in a Christian, Muslim, or Jewish sense. Again, I know it's scary to grow up believing in something that can turn out to not be true. And read the entire bible, it's a sometimes dry but insane read. 
1|0@DebateAChristian^1440355839#cud1ivy:heyvina$:I was raised by a mother who converted to Christianity when I was a toddler, and a strictly atheist father who was raised by a single mother and abusive father. I had both ends of the spectrum for my upbringing and it was difficult navigating the contrary opinions and strife their not being on the same page resulted in. I have 'been on fire for Jesus' I have been convinced there is no god, no creator and strived on my own ability. I worked hard for many years to put away any preconceptions I had and look at things objectively. To understand why people believe what they do. What circumstances led them to where they are and to challenge everything I think I know. Now I've settled somewhere in the middle. But most importantly I am done with the stage of bitterness towards Christians for misrepresenting the source of all and therefore assuming there isn't one in one facet or another. That feeling of being tricked is powerful, and many people react to the polar opposite side (with good reason). I would agree with your statement of no evidence that God exists, especially in a Christian, Jewish or Muslim sense. You sensed something was off. Most people do and eventually have to come to terms with it. I am convinced however, that the fact that over the course of humanity, we have always found the need for a higher power and the need to explain it is quite telling. I think we are at a point where it is possible to possibly accept that there may be more of a purpose and design to our lives than we currently understand, but not to allow flawed and selfish people to tell us exactly what that is. I wrote a comment in another thread on my thoughts of how to access it, how I did when I was convinced there was no source. Let me know and I'll share it with you, it opened my eyes and my life to freedom (I know how stupid and corny it sounds, but don't knock it till you've tried it:)
1|0@DebateAChristian^1440372633#cudb416:Mr-Toy$:Interesting. Of course, please share a link if you have one. 
1|0@DebateAChristian^1440374197#cudbz20:heyvina$:This is what I started doing when convinced there was no God who took a personal interest in my life. Copied and pasted. It's usually met with ridicule and unwillingness to even consider something so trite, but it opened my eyes. First write down one thing or a list of things in your life you would like to overcome. I'm talking along the lines of jealousy, anger, fear, worry, bitterness Anything that most religions would collectively say takes away from freedom. And more importantly anything that you can recognize would make your life more peaceful, more content, more full of joy. Next, we will play pretend. There are three sources we will attribute to. Self. Darkness. Light. Those thoughts pertaining to the thing we will categorically file under. If the thought 'I want to fuck this person' enters the mind, we will file it under self. If a thought of 'I am ugly, I am worthless, I cannot accomplish this because I will fail' enters the thoughts, we file it under darkness. Thoughts of love and selflessness we will file under light. 'Instead of going home and playing video games i should grab a drink with the new dude at work who has no friends' 'life is beautiful and the sky is fucking sweet' 'I should show grace to frank' Obviously it will take time to understand where to appropriate certain thoughts, and that is part of the process. Your thoughts on each level are going to be different from the examples I gave, but I'll let you use your own discernment, you know what the categories are. The next step is to work on CHOOSING to ignore, banish, fight, meditate away, *all thoughts except for ones of light. You may want to come up with a mantra. You may need to scream out in your mind (or aloud) FUCKING HELP ME. Meditation helps and is worth looking into. But every time one of these thoughts intrudes in your thought land, you will consider it an intruder. You will fight to make it leave, make it aware that it has no place, no purpose and is not welcome. It will take time. I recommend doing this mind experiment for at least a month to begin. And you can treat it as such- only an experiment. You have nothing to lose. I'm not asking you to believe in some deity. Only to try this process and see what it opens up in your mind. My experience is such that at first I become finally attuned to the power that I have to create within my mind the kind of environment I want. That's not a crazy or religious thing, it's brain training, neuroplasticity and epigenics. But with the training, the habitual fighting of the thoughts of self and darkness, comes another still small voice that is amplified and comes more regularly in time. The most I listened to that voice, the more I began to realize I did not understand it. Yes, it was me. I first called it my intuition and marveled at my capacity for intellect and figuring out patterns. But as it grew more prevalent, as I gave it freedom to direct me, it became more apparent that while yes, it was me, it is something that exists in everything, and it has a purpose. It sounds hokey I agree, but worth trying if you're bored and like experiments with no danger:)
