1|0@DebateAChristian^1440340409#cuctidt:Autodidact2$:To me, it's wrong, and I mean morally wrong, to base beliefs on anything other than evidence. I'm sure you agree that our minds are capable of experiencing many things without any external reality behind them. If Christians stuck to saying these are feelings they have in their minds, we wouldn't have a problem with it. But they don't, do they? They make factual claims about the physical world, and moral claims about how people should act. I don't think we should base our actions or beliefs on feelings you have in your mind, do you?
1|0@DebateAChristian^1440343562#cucuy7z:waterless$:&gt;To me, it's wrong, and I mean morally wrong, to base beliefs on anything other than evidence. &gt; &gt;I'm sure you agree that our minds are capable of experiencing many things without any external reality behind them. If Christians stuck to saying these are feelings they have in their minds, we wouldn't have a problem with it. But they don't, do they? They make factual claims about the physical world, and moral claims about how people should act. I don't think we should base our actions or beliefs on feelings you have in your mind, do you? Slight seque but feelings in the mind do say something about reality, e.g., that a model of reality that doesn't contain what consciousness is (and not just with what measurable phenomena it's associated) is incomplete. But it all depends on the specific kind of religious claims being made. Saying God exists and loves you isn't necessarily a statement about the content of the physical world, but about something beyond that world; in contrast, saying God will do anything you ask for in prayer, on the other hand, is. I'd have a problem with the latter, since it violates our best tools for reasoning about causality, but the former is more axiomatic, a worldview, about how you perceive or interpret the data rather than data themselves. You can't avoid worldview-level axioms, whether they're theistic or naturalistic, and you can't apply principles relevant to empirical claims to them. If you prefer naturalism it's not because a scientist provided empirical proof of existence, it's because you have a preference for its expected consequences, given contextual factors including social ones. Same for theism. 
1|0@DebateAChristian^1440344020#cucv66r:Autodidact2$:I agree. The fact that we are conscious does tell us that reality includes consciousness, and some other things. It doesn't tell us whether those sensations are caused by outside stimuli. &gt;Saying God exists and loves you isn't necessarily a statement about the content of the physical world, but about something beyond that world If it's not in the world I live in, where is it, and how does it affect me? And, more importantly, how could you possibly know? &gt; saying God will do anything you ask for in prayer, on the other hand, is. The Christian God does claim to do this, repeatedly, so we can agree that God does not exist. &gt; it violates our best tools for reasoning about causality Exactly. It appears that God does not exist. Now, a God who exists "beyond the world," sure, who knows or cares? Such a God would make no difference or impact in my life. &gt; If you prefer naturalism it's not because a scientist provided empirical proof of existence, it's because you have a preference for its expected consequences, given contextual factors including social ones. Actually, it's because that's what I believe meets the definition of "existing." To say that some "exists" "beyond this world" is the functional equivalent of saying that it doesn't exist
1|0@DebateAChristian^1440345375#cucvu3d:waterless$:&gt; I agree. The fact that we are conscious does tell us that reality includes consciousness, and some other things. It doesn't tell us whether those sensations are caused by outside stimuli. We agree there! &gt; The Christian God does claim to do this, repeatedly, so we can agree that God does not exist. We could agree that the way humans have described God as acting as a causal agent within the universe is due to their limited point of view. &gt; Now, a God who exists "beyond the world," sure, who knows or cares? Such a God would make no difference or impact in my life. That may be the case for you, but not for everyone - in the sense that belief in such a God can have a huge effect on you and your behavior. On your question how we would know, again, we're talking about worldviews, not data points. It's a way of perceiving reality. Don't make the category error of trying to force it into the framework of empirically testing hypotheses. &gt; Actually, it's because that's what I believe meets the definition of "existing." To say that some "exists" "beyond this world" is the functional equivalent of saying that it doesn't exist Edit: haha, sorry, this part is because I mixed up which thread we were in, there's another one about teaching children about God, that's what I was on about here. // I'd put it to you that imposing that way of thinking on a child would definitely be extremely harmful since it's hugely intellectually limiting. A child brought up with the idea of a God could reject it, but if someone can't even conceive of things existing in some sense beyond the limits of a stricty naturalistic worldview it would be far harder for them to ever even understand there are alternatives. 
